Why this dataset carries a data licence rather than a code licence

The mapping from cryptographic algorithm to standards obligation is a factual
dataset with provenance: every entry records the primary source it was read from,
the date it was retrieved, and whether it is `verified` or `needs-check`. See
README.md in this directory.

Being copied is the intended outcome. Attribution is the only condition, because
the value here is in being the reference dataset other tools cite — including the
cryptographic-discovery tools NIST SP 1800-38B convened. Standards decay, and a
dataset that invites correction stays accurate longer than one that does not.

ATTRIBUTION AND ACCURACY

Attribute as: "QuantaXscan PQC mappings dataset, version <dataVersion>", quoting
the `dataVersion` field of the file you used.

Entries marked `needs-check` have NOT been verified against a primary source. They
are published so the gap is visible, not because they are reliable. Do not present
a `needs-check` entry as a verified standards claim, and do not strip the
verification status when redistributing — it is part of the data.

NO WARRANTY. This dataset is not legal or compliance advice. Verify against the
primary source before relying on it in a regulatory submission.
